.TH rx 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
rx, sx \- receive and send a file with XMODEM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rx
[\fB\-t\fI tty\fR] [\fB\-b\fI baud\fR] [\fB\-f\fR] \fIfilename\fR
.br
.B sx
[\fB\-t\fI tty\fR] [\fB\-b\fI baud\fR] \fIfilename\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rx
receives a file over a serial port with the XMODEM protocol and writes
it to
.IR filename ;
.B sx
sends one.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-t " tty"
The port to use.  The default is
.BR /dev/tty2 ,
which on this machine is the second serial port and not the console.
.TP
.BI \-b " baud"
The speed to set on that port.
.TP
.B \-f
Overwrite an existing file.
.SH NOTES
These need a serial port that is
.I not
the console.  They watch the console for a keypress so that a transfer
can be cancelled, so on a machine where the console is the link the
first byte of the transfer reads as a cancel.
.PP
Over the console itself, use
.BR uue (1)
and
.BR uud (1),
which send printable text through the line discipline and need no
second port.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR uue (1),
.BR fat (1),
.BR stty (1)
